You could use a set with a custom comparator to check for duplicated vertices with a tolerance.
https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/master/core/templates/set.h

The default comparator is this one:
https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/6f8d5cff66416993856bdb01c83b999b84425fb0/core/typedefs.h#L232-L236

This comparator should do the trick (I haven't tested though):
```
#define DUPLICATE_VERT_EPSILON 0.001f
struct Comparator { 
    bool operator()(const Vector3 &p_a, const Vector3 &p_b) const
    {
        if (Math::is_equal_approx(p_a.x, p_b.x, DUPLICATE_VERT_EPSILON)) {
		if (Math::is_equal_approx(p_a.y, p_b.y, DUPLICATE_VERT_EPSILON)) {
			return p_a.z < p_b.z - DUPLICATE_VERT_EPSILON;
		}
		return p_a.y < p_b.y - DUPLICATE_VERT_EPSILON;
	}
	return p_a.x < p_b.x - DUPLICATE_VERT_EPSILON;
    } 
};
```

**Edit:** updated comparator with a custom tolerance instead of using CMP_EPSILON to catch very close vertices that can be considered duplicates. In the future, we could also add an option in the editor to set the tolerance.

@mortarroad I'm not sure I understand what you mean by working only in one dimension but I'm interested to learn. Is there more explanation or an external source you can share?

In any case, I wonder now if the best solution could be to just use VHACD for single collision shapes, since we're already using it in the editor for multiple convex shapes. We can just set it to one convex max for this case. It does a very good job at simplifying vertices without messing with the volume.

It would need a bit more testing, but when I did a quick test while looking into #48468, it seemed to work fine this way.
